    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **45 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned owner identity so the `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>` and every value derived from it moves to the new identity.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 rewrote the code assignment. It now builds `<REGION>-<HASH8>` where:\n   - `REGION` = the region derived from the postcode via the shared `Localities.region(city, postcode)` derivation (postcode-range first, city fallback);\n   - `HASH8` = the first 8 upper-case hex characters of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)` \u2014 the telephone is already normalized to E.164 by the time this step runs.\n   - The city-prefix, last-name-prefix, and per-city sequence number (and the repository scan they required) are gone.\n\n2. **`Localities.java`** \u2014 added `region(Owner)`, which reads the locality from the `REGION` component of the owner's `customerCode` (falling back to the postcode/city derivation for owners without a code), so locality is now derived from the region-and-hash identity itself.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 pointed the `locality` mapping at the new `Localities.region(owner)`.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `customerCode` schema description and example to the new format.\n\n**Automatically follow the new identity (no code change needed, since they already read `customerCode`):** the membership number (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`), its Luhn check digit, and the create audit line \u2014 the audit output confirms lines like `customerCode=NSW-F4F6FF62`.",
